// One array renders the visible FAQ and the FAQPage node.
const faqs: Faq[] = [
  { q: "What is Shopify server-side tracking and how does it work?",
    a: "<p>Shopify server-side tracking sends purchase events directly from your server to ad platforms without going through the customer’s browser. When a Shopify order is paid, Shopify sends an order webhook to Make.com, which posts the purchase to Meta’s Conversions API and TikTok’s Events API, so ad blockers and browser privacy limits cannot stop it. For Google Ads, the free route is enhanced conversions in Shopify’s Google & YouTube app. It runs alongside your browser pixel, not instead of it.</p>" },
  { q: "Do I need to remove my browser pixel when setting up server-side tracking?",
    a: "<p>No: keep your browser pixel running alongside server-side tracking. The pixel captures upper-funnel events (ViewContent, AddToCart, InitiateCheckout) that order webhooks don’t cover, since they only fire on payment. Whether the platform merges a browser event and a server event depends on both carrying the same event_id, and Shopify does not document the event_id its own pixels send, so check Test Events after a test order rather than assuming it.</p>" },
  { q: "How much of my lost Shopify conversion data will server-side tracking recover?",
    a: "<p>We publish no recovery percentage, and no honest one exists as an industry figure: what server-side tracking recovers is whatever your browser pixel was losing, which is set by your own mobile/iOS traffic mix and consent rate. Measure it as Additional Conversions Reported in Meta Events Manager, or with the <a href=\"/shopify-vs-meta-attribution-gap-calculator/\">attribution gap calculator</a>, and see <a href=\"/how-we-test/#the-20-40-figure\">why the 20–40% estimate was retracted</a>. Meta reported in April 2026 that advertisers running the Conversions API for web events saw an average 17.8% lower cost per result versus those without it; that is Meta's figure across its advertisers, not a measurement of your store.</p>" },
  { q: "Is server-side tracking GDPR compliant?",
    a: "<p>The data flows through your own Make.com workspace: you control what is sent and can delete the scenario at any time. All personally identifiable information (email, phone) is SHA-256 hashed before transmission as required by each platform’s API specification. Ensure your store privacy policy discloses server-side conversion data sharing with advertising platforms. For GDPR-specific advice consult a legal professional.</p>" },
  { q: "How long before I see results after setting up server-side tracking?",
    a: "<p>Server events start with the first paid order after you switch the scenario on, and appear in Meta’s and TikTok’s Test Events within minutes. Reported conversions update in each platform’s normal reporting delay. We publish no figure for how soon cost per purchase changes: judge it on several weeks of results, against the same weeks before the change.</p>" },
  { q: "How often does Make.com’s free tier actually fail or rate-limit?",
    a: "<p>The Make.com free plan includes 1,000 credits a month. Most modules use one credit each time they run, so this guide’s scenario (webhook, Meta, TikTok) uses about three credits per order; your scenario history shows the real figure. When credits run out, scenarios stop until the next allowance. The failures that matter more are a platform API rejecting a request, an expired token or a malformed payload. Add a Break error handler and turn on storing incomplete executions, so a rejected request is kept for retry instead of disappearing; a re-run uses credits again.</p>" },
  { q: "Will server-side tracking keep working as iOS tightens privacy further?",
    a: "<p>Yes. Server-side tracking is specifically designed to be immune to iOS browser-level restrictions because the event originates from Shopify’s server, not the customer’s iPhone. Link Tracking Protection (iOS 17 and later) and Safari’s Intelligent Tracking Prevention act on links and cookies in the browser, and an order webhook does not pass through either. What browser privacy does still affect is matching: if a click ID was stripped, the platform has only the hashed customer data to match on.</p>" },
  { q: "Do I still need server-side tracking if I’m using Shopify’s native Customer Events / Web Pixels?",
    a: "<p>Yes. Shopify’s Web Pixels framework is still browser-based: it loads in a sandboxed iframe in the customer’s browser and is subject to the same ad blocker and ITP restrictions as a regular pixel. A Shopify order webhook, by contrast, fires from Shopify’s backend when an order is paid and does not depend on the browser. Web Pixels remain the way to send browsing events (PageView, AddToCart); a server route is what makes the Purchase independent of the browser.</p>" },
  { q: "What is the difference between Meta CAPI and Shopify’s native data sharing?",
    a: "<p>Shopify’s help centre says its Facebook &amp; Instagram app uses Meta’s Conversions API at the Enhanced and Maximum data sharing levels, and for a Meta-only store that may be all you need. Shopify does not document exactly what triggers each server event. The Make.com webhook method is triggered by Shopify’s order webhook with no browser involved, and the same trigger can feed TikTok. Use one server route for Meta, not both, unless you have confirmed their event IDs match.</p>" },
  { q: "Can I set up Shopify server-side tracking without a developer?",
    a: "<p>Yes. The Make.com webhook setup requires no code. You configure a Shopify webhook in Settings, build an HTTP module in Make.com’s visual drag-and-drop interface, and paste your API credentials. No developer, agency or Shopify app is required, but test each step with a real order before relying on it.</p>" },
  { q: "Does server-side tracking work with Shopify’s native Maximum data sharing?",
    a: "<p>It works, but do not run both for Meta. Shopify’s help centre says its Facebook &amp; Instagram app already uses the Conversions API at the Enhanced and Maximum data sharing levels, so adding a webhook route for Meta can send the same purchase twice. Choose one Meta server route: Shopify’s, or the Make.com scenario with Shopify’s data sharing set below Enhanced. The Make.com route is still useful for TikTok.</p>" },
  { q: "How much does Shopify server-side tracking cost?",
    a: "<p>The Make.com webhook method costs nothing within Make.com’s free plan of 1,000 credits a month; this guide’s scenario uses about one credit per module per order, and your scenario history shows the real figure. Above that, the Make.com Core plan at $9/month covers 10,000 credits (<a href=\"https://www.make.com/en/pricing\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">make.com/en/pricing</a>, read 25 September 2026). Managed alternatives like Elevar (from $225/month) and Triple Whale (priced by store revenue) achieve the same tracking quality at significantly higher cost. The premium pays for managed onboarding and dashboards, not for superior API access.</p>" },
  { q: "What Event Match Quality (EMQ) score is good for Meta CAPI on Shopify?",
    a: "<p>Meta shows Event Match Quality as a score out of 10 for each event in Events Manager, calculated from the match keys the event carries. We publish no target score, because we have not sampled it across stores. A Shopify order webhook can supply hashed email, phone, name, city, zip and country, plus the customer’s IP address, user agent and a hashed <code>external_id</code> (the customer ID). It cannot supply <code>fbc</code> or <code>fbp</code>, which come from the browser. If your score is lower than you expect, check email format (lowercase and trim before SHA-256), phone format (E.164 before SHA-256) and that IP and user agent come from the order, not from Make.com.</p>" },
  { q: "Why is my TikTok event match quality low even with server-side tracking?",
    a: "<p>The single most common cause is sending the event name “Purchase” instead of <code>CompletePayment</code>. TikTok’s Events API requires <code>CompletePayment</code> as the event name for purchase events: sending “Purchase” (which works for Meta) results in low Event Match Score and prevents purchase-campaign optimisation. Other common causes: missing hashed email, missing IP address (you must map <code>order.client_details.browser_ip</code> from the Shopify webhook payload, not the Make.com runtime IP), missing TikTok Click ID (<code>ttclid</code>) for paid traffic, and missing user agent. Send all four high-impact signals (email, phone, IP/UA, external_id): that is the full set TikTok calculates the score from. We publish no figure for what it scores; read yours in TikTok Events Manager.</p>" },
  { q: "Is Shopify server-side tracking free?",
    a: "<p>Yes: the tracking itself is free to implement. The <a href=\"#setup\">Make.com webhook method</a> in this guide costs <strong>$0/month</strong> within Make.com’s free 1,000 credits; about one credit per module per order. Above that, Make.com Core is $9/month for 10,000 credits. Meta’s Conversions API and TikTok’s Events API are free to call, and enhanced conversions in Shopify’s Google &amp; YouTube app costs nothing. You only pay if you choose a <em>managed</em> tool: Elevar runs from $225/month and Triple Whale prices by store revenue, but they charge for dashboards and managed onboarding, not for better tracking access. The same events reach the same endpoints either way. See the <a href=\"#comparison\">free vs paid comparison</a> for the full breakdown.</p>" },
  { q: "Shopify server-side tracking vs Elevar: what’s the difference?",
    a: "<p>Both send server-side purchase events; the difference is who builds and maintains the pipeline, and what it covers. <strong>Elevar</strong> is a managed service from $225/month: it provisions a server-side GTM container, supplies a pre-built Shopify data layer, monitors delivery with alerts, and handles refunds and order edits automatically. The <strong>free Make.com webhook method</strong> sends the purchase to Meta and TikTok from the order webhook, but you build and maintain it yourself, and it cannot capture a Google click ID. Choose Make.com for zero cost and full control; choose Elevar if managed monitoring, automatic edge-case handling, and a reporting dashboard justify the monthly fee at your ad spend. </p>" },
  { q: "Do I need server-side GTM (sGTM) for Shopify server-side tracking?",
    a: "<p>No. Server-side Google Tag Manager is <em>one</em> way to do server-side tracking, but it requires provisioning and paying for a Google Cloud tagging server and configuring containers: overkill for most Shopify stores. The Make.com webhook method skips GTM entirely: Shopify fires an order webhook directly to Make.com, which POSTs to each platform’s API. You get the same server-to-server delivery with no server to host or maintain. sGTM earns its complexity only for large stores with dedicated analytics engineers needing custom tag logic across many destinations; for everyone else, the webhook method is simpler and free for purchase events.</p>" },
];
