    <h1>Shopify Server-Side Tracking Setup<br>Meta, TikTok &amp; Google: <span class="g">step by step</span></h1>
    <p id="answer">Shopify server-side tracking sends each paid order to the ad platforms from Shopify's servers, not the shopper's browser. A free Make.com scenario receives Shopify's order webhook, hashes the customer data and posts the purchase to Meta and TikTok. For Google Ads, turn on enhanced conversions in Shopify's Google &amp; YouTube app instead.</p>
<p style="font-size:14px;color:#93a09a;line-height:1.7;margin:10px 0 18px;">The pieces, in each platform&rsquo;s own words (checked 24 September 2026): Shopify sends the <a href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/webhooks" target="_blank" rel="noopener">orders/paid webhook</a> whenever an order is paid; Meta requires contact details to be <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/documentation/ads-commerce/conversions-api/parameters/customer-information-parameters" target="_blank" rel="noopener">normalised and SHA256-hashed</a>; Google&rsquo;s <a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/9888656" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Enhanced Conversions</a> sends SHA256-hashed first-party data alongside the conversion; and TikTok recommends running the <a href="https://ads.tiktok.com/help/article/events-api" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Events API</a> alongside its Pixel.</p>
    <p class="hero-deck">Shopify records every paid order; your ad platforms only count the purchases their tags or servers tell them about. <strong>The gap is not a reporting quirk you can annotate away: it is the training data each platform uses to decide who sees your ads next.</strong> Server-side events close the part of it the browser causes.</p>
