    <h2 id="shopify-server-side-tracking-explained-in-60-seconds">Shopify Server-Side Tracking Explained in 60 Seconds</h2>
    <p><strong>Shopify server-side tracking is a method of sending purchase events from Shopify&rsquo;s backend to the ad platforms, instead of relying on a tag in the customer&rsquo;s browser.</strong> Browser tags can be stopped by ad blockers, declined consent and browser privacy features, and a server-side event cannot. We publish no figure for how many purchases a browser tag misses: it depends on your own traffic and consent rate, so no industry range describes your store. <a href="/shopify-vs-meta-attribution-gap-calculator/">Measure yours</a>.</p>
    <p>The mechanism is straightforward. When a Shopify order is paid, Shopify sends an <code>orders/paid</code> webhook to a Make.com scenario. The order data includes the customer&rsquo;s email, phone, name and address, the order total and currency, and the IP address and user agent of the browser that placed the order. Make.com hashes the personal data with SHA-256 and posts a purchase event to Meta&rsquo;s Conversions API and TikTok&rsquo;s Events API. Google is different: Shopify&rsquo;s order data has no Google click ID, so for most stores the free route is enhanced conversions in Shopify&rsquo;s Google &amp; YouTube app.</p>
    <p>A server-side event carries match keys the browser tag may not have, and each platform&rsquo;s match score is calculated from the keys present. We have not sampled scores before and after across stores, so we publish no figure for either side: read yours in each platform&rsquo;s events manager.</p>

    <h3 id="quick-answer-table">Quick answer table</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>What it is:</strong> Server-to-server transmission of conversion events, fired by Shopify webhooks instead of browser JavaScript.</li>
      <li><strong>Why it&rsquo;s needed in 2026:</strong> iOS App Tracking Transparency, Link Tracking Protection (iOS 17 and later, in Mail, Messages and Private Browsing), Safari&rsquo;s cookie limits, ad blockers and declined consent all stop some browser tag events. How many is a property of your store.</li>
      <li><strong>What it costs:</strong> $0/month using the Make.com webhook method. From $145/month using managed services (Analyzify, Triple Whale, Elevar).</li>
      <li><strong>What it takes:</strong> one Shopify webhook and a two-module Make.com scenario, or a server-side Google Tag Manager container on Google Cloud if you need custom tag logic.</li>
      <li><strong>What you need:</strong> Shopify Admin access, a Meta Conversions API access token, a TikTok Events API access token, a free Make.com account, and Shopify&rsquo;s Google &amp; YouTube app for Google Ads.</li>
      <li><strong>What you should send:</strong> <code>Purchase</code> (Meta) and <code>CompletePayment</code> (TikTok). Note: TikTok does not use &ldquo;Purchase&rdquo;. Sending it is the most common setup error.</li>
      <li><strong>What changes:</strong> events that browser tracking loses are delivered server-side instead, and each event carries more match keys. We have not measured the effect on reported conversions, EMQ or cost-per-purchase for any store, so this guide does not quote figures for them: you can read all three in your own Events Manager before and after.</li>
    </ul>

  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 1: WHAT IS -->
  <div class="sec-mark" id="what-is">DEFINITION</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="what-is-server-side-tagging-shopify-server-side-tracking">What is Server Side Tagging (Shopify Server-Side Tracking)?</h2>
    <p>Server-side tracking (also called Conversions API, server-to-server tracking, or S2S tracking) sends conversion events directly from your web server to advertising platforms: completely bypassing the customer&rsquo;s browser. Instead of relying on a JavaScript pixel in the browser to fire when a purchase completes, a webhook fires from Shopify&rsquo;s backend when payment is confirmed, and an automation posts the purchase to the platform&rsquo;s server API, such as Meta&rsquo;s Conversions API or TikTok&rsquo;s Events API.</p>
    <p>The key distinction is where the event originates. A browser pixel depends on the customer&rsquo;s device: their browser settings, installed extensions, network conditions, and whether they accepted your cookie banner. A server event originates from Shopify&rsquo;s infrastructure, so ad blockers, iOS privacy restrictions and Safari&rsquo;s Intelligent Tracking Prevention cannot stop it from being sent. Whether the platform can match it to an ad click is a separate question, answered by the match keys it carries.</p>
    <h3 id="server-side-vs-browser-pixel-the-architecture">Server-side vs browser pixel: the architecture</h3>
  </div>
  <figure style="max-width:960px;margin:28px auto;border:1px solid #2f2f2f;border-radius:18px;overflow:hidden;background:#0b0e0c;">
    <img src="/diagrams/shopify-server-side-tracking-flow-2026.svg"
         alt="Shopify server-side tracking architecture diagram 2026: one Shopify orders/paid webhook feeds a single Make.com scenario that hashes customer data and sends server-to-server events to Meta Conversions API and TikTok Events API, with Google Ads handled by enhanced conversions in Shopify's Google &amp; YouTube app"
         width="1200" height="630" loading="lazy" decoding="async" style="display:block;width:100%;height:auto;" />
