  <!-- SECTION 5: META CAPI -->
  <div class="sec-mark" id="meta-capi">META CAPI</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="meta-conversions-api-complete-configuration">Meta Conversions API: Complete Configuration</h2>
    <p>Meta&rsquo;s Conversions API (CAPI) is the server-side counterpart to the Meta Pixel. It was introduced after Apple&rsquo;s iOS 14.5 ATT update and has since become Meta&rsquo;s primary recommended tracking method for e-commerce. When your browser pixel and a CAPI event report the same purchase with the same <span class="code-ref">event_id</span>, Meta counts one conversion. The full <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/docs/marketing-api/conversions-api" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Meta Conversions API documentation</a> covers all available parameters and the required fields for e-commerce events.
    <h3 id="the-exact-meta-capi-payload">The exact Meta CAPI payload</h3>
    <p>Every field below improves your Event Match Quality score. The more fields you send, the better Meta can match the event to a real user in its database and attribute the conversion correctly.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="callout sky">
    <span class="callout-label">Full Meta CAPI payload structure</span>
    <p><strong>Endpoint:</strong> POST https://graph.facebook.com/v24.0/&#123;PIXEL_ID&#125;/events?access_token=&#123;TOKEN&#125; <em style="color:#93a09a;">(use the current Graph API version: see Meta&rsquo;s <a href="https://developers.facebook.com/docs/graph-api/changelog/" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:#93a09a;text-decoration:underline;">changelog</a>)</em><br><br>
    Required: <span class="code-ref">event_name</span> Purchase &bull; <span class="code-ref">event_time</span> Unix timestamp &bull; <span class="code-ref">action_source</span> website &bull; <span class="code-ref">event_id</span> Shopify order ID<br><br>
    User data (all SHA-256 hashed): <span class="code-ref">em</span> email &bull; <span class="code-ref">ph</span> phone &bull; <span class="code-ref">fn</span> first name &bull; <span class="code-ref">ln</span> last name &bull; <span class="code-ref">ct</span> city &bull; <span class="code-ref">zp</span> zip code &bull; <span class="code-ref">country</span> 2-letter code &bull; <span class="code-ref">client_ip_address</span> raw IP &bull; <span class="code-ref">client_user_agent</span> raw UA<br><br>
    Custom data: <span class="code-ref">currency</span> &bull; <span class="code-ref">value</span> &bull; <span class="code-ref">order_id</span> &bull; <span class="code-ref">content_ids</span> array of SKUs &bull; <span class="code-ref">num_items</span> quantity</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3 id="deduplication-the-most-important-step">Deduplication: the most important step</h3>
    <p>Two things can count one purchase twice. The first is two server routes: Shopify&rsquo;s Facebook &amp; Instagram app sends the Conversions API at its Enhanced and Maximum data sharing levels, so adding this scenario&rsquo;s Meta module as well can send each purchase twice. Use one. The second is a browser and a server event with different IDs. Meta merges them only when both carry the same <span class="code-ref">event_id</span>. This scenario uses the Shopify order ID, and Shopify does not document the ID its pixel sends, so do not assume they match: after a test order, open the Purchase event in Test Events and check whether Meta shows it as deduplicated.</p>
    <h3 id="testing-meta-capi">Testing Meta CAPI</h3>
    <p>In Meta Events Manager, navigate to <strong>Test Events</strong>. You will see a Test Event Code. Add this as a <span class="code-ref">test_event_code</span> parameter in your Make.com HTTP module during testing. Place a real test order. You should see a Purchase event appear with its source labelled &ldquo;Server&rdquo;. Once confirmed, remove the test_event_code from your production scenario.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 6: GOOGLE -->
  <div class="sec-mark" id="google-enhanced">GOOGLE ENHANCED CONVERSIONS</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="google-enhanced-conversions-setup-verification">Google Enhanced Conversions: Setup &amp; Verification</h2>
    <p>Google enhanced conversions sends hashed customer data, such as email address and phone number, with the conversions your Google tag records, so Google can match more of them to signed-in Google accounts when the click ID has been lost (<a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/9888656" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Ads Help</a>). It improves matching for purchases the tag records; it cannot report a purchase whose tag never ran.</p>
    <p>For most Shopify stores the free way to turn it on is in Shopify&rsquo;s Google &amp; YouTube app: <strong>Settings &rarr; Google Ads settings &rarr; Enhanced conversions &rarr; Turn on</strong>, then accept Google&rsquo;s customer data terms (<a href="https://support.google.com/google-ads/answer/13494537" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google Ads Help</a>). It needs no Make.com scenario. A Make.com upload is weaker for two reasons: Shopify&rsquo;s order data has no Google click ID, so the upload carries only hashed customer data to match on, and Google is moving its click conversion upload from the Google Ads API to its Data Manager API. The <a href="/shopify-google-ads-conversion-tracking/">Google Ads conversion tracking guide</a> covers both routes.</p>
    <h3 id="data-fields-that-improve-google-match-rate">Data fields that improve Google match rate</h3>
    <p>The Google &amp; YouTube app takes these from the checkout for you. They matter if you build your own route:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="emq-grid">
    <div class="emq-item high"><span class="emq-label">High impact</span><div class="emq-name">Email (SHA-256)</div><div class="emq-note">Primary match key. Lowercase and trim, then hash.</div></div>
    <div class="emq-item high"><span class="emq-label">High impact</span><div class="emq-name">Phone (SHA-256)</div><div class="emq-note">E.164 format (+447911123456), then hash.</div></div>
    <div class="emq-item mid"><span class="emq-label">Medium impact</span><div class="emq-name">First + Last Name</div><div class="emq-note">Lowercase and trim, then hash. Sent with a postal address.</div></div>
    <div class="emq-item mid"><span class="emq-label">Medium impact</span><div class="emq-name">Postal address</div><div class="emq-note">Street, city, postal code and country.</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="callout">
    <span class="callout-label">SHA-256 hashing in Make.com</span>
    <p>In Make.com, hash with <span class="code-ref">sha256(lower(trim(email)))</span>. Apply the same normalise-then-hash pattern to every personal field before sending. Meta and TikTok require hashed contact details; IP address and user agent are sent unhashed.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h3 id="verifying-google-enhanced-conversions">Verifying Google Enhanced Conversions</h3>
    <p>In Google Ads, open <strong>Goals &rarr; Conversions</strong>, select your purchase conversion action and open its diagnostics, which report the enhanced conversions status. Then compare Google Ads conversions with Shopify orders over the same two weeks. Some gap always remains, because not every buyer can be matched and not every order came from an ad.</p>
    <p class="callout-inline">Prefer a ready-made version for Meta? <a href="/capi-shield/"><strong>CAPI Shield</strong></a> is the pre-built Make.com scenario for Meta CAPI.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 7: TIKTOK -->
  <div class="sec-mark" id="tiktok-events">TIKTOK EVENTS API</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="tiktok-events-api-maximising-event-match-quality">TikTok Events API: Maximising Event Match Quality</h2>
    <p>TikTok shows a match quality score for the events you send in TikTok Events Manager, calculated from the customer data each event carries. We publish no target for it. The more of the right keys an event carries, the more TikTok can match it to a TikTok user and use it to optimise delivery.</p>
    <p>The single most important difference between TikTok and Meta/Google: TikTok requires the event name <strong>CompletePayment</strong>, not &ldquo;Purchase&rdquo;. If you send &ldquo;Purchase&rdquo; to TikTok&rsquo;s Events API, it will appear in your Events Manager but will not be used for purchase campaign optimisation. This is the most common TikTok setup mistake.</p>
    <h3 id="tiktok-emq-signal-hierarchy">TikTok EMQ signal hierarchy</h3>
  </div>
  <div class="emq-grid">
    <div class="emq-item high"><span class="emq-label">Critical</span><div class="emq-name">Email (SHA-256)</div><div class="emq-note">Strongest matching signal. Send as lowercase SHA-256 hash.</div></div>
    <div class="emq-item high"><span class="emq-label">Critical</span><div class="emq-name">Phone (SHA-256)</div><div class="emq-note">E.164 format, then SHA-256.</div></div>
    <div class="emq-item high"><span class="emq-label">High</span><div class="emq-name">External ID</div><div class="emq-note">Your internal customer ID from Shopify. Hashed SHA-256.</div></div>
    <div class="emq-item mid"><span class="emq-label">Medium</span><div class="emq-name">IP + User Agent</div><div class="emq-note">From the order&rsquo;s client_details, sent unhashed.</div></div>
  </div>
  <div class="callout tiktok">
    <span class="callout-label">TikTok API endpoint</span>
    <p><strong>POST</strong> https://business-api.tiktok.com/open_api/v1.3/event/track/<br>Headers: <span class="code-ref">Access-Token: YOUR_TOKEN</span> &bull; <span class="code-ref">Content-Type: application/json</span><br>Body: <span class="code-ref">event_source</span> web &bull; <span class="code-ref">event_source_id</span> your pixel ID &bull; and in <span class="code-ref">data[]</span>: <span class="code-ref">event</span> CompletePayment &bull; <span class="code-ref">event_time</span> Unix seconds &bull; <span class="code-ref">event_id</span> order ID &bull; <span class="code-ref">user</span> with hashed email and phone, ip and user_agent &bull; <span class="code-ref">properties</span> value and currency</p>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Send all four high-impact signals (email, phone, external_id, IP/UA); that is the full set TikTok calculates the score from, and we publish no figure for what it scores. For context on server-side events in general, Meta reported in April 2026 that advertisers using its Conversions API for web events saw an average 17.8% lower cost per result than those without it; that is Meta&rsquo;s figure for Meta, not a measurement of TikTok or of your store. Check your EMQ score in TikTok Events Manager &rarr; your pixel &rarr; Overview &rarr; Event Match Quality.</p>
    <p class="callout-inline">The pre-built TikTok version is <a href="/tiktok-events-api-shopify/"><strong>TikTok Events API for Shopify</strong></a>: the same CompletePayment setup with the full request body.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 8: COMPARISON -->
  <div class="sec-mark" id="comparison">FREE VS PAID COMPARISON</div>
  <div class="prose"><h2 id="makecom-free-vs-elevar-vs-triple-whale-vs-analyzify">Make.com Free vs Elevar vs Triple Whale vs Analyzify</h2><p>The question is whether you need to pay for a managed solution, or whether the free Make.com method covers what you need. They are not equivalent: managed tools handle refunds, order edits and monitoring that a two-module scenario does not. Here is the comparison.</p></div>
  <div class="comp-table-wrap">
    <table class="comp-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Solution</th><th>Cost/month</th><th>Platforms</th><th>Setup</th><th>Reliability</th><th>Support</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="col-label">Make.com (this guide)</td><td class="col-good">$0</td><td>Meta + TikTok (Google via the Google &amp; YouTube app)</td><td>DIY, no code</td><td>Depends on your error handling</td><td>Community + this guide</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="col-label">Elevar</td><td class="col-bad">from $225/mo</td><td>Meta + Google + TikTok + more</td><td>Managed onboarding</td><td class="col-good">&#x2713; Excellent</td><td>Dedicated account manager</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="col-label">Triple Whale</td><td class="col-bad">Priced by store revenue (not published)</td><td>Meta + Google + TikTok</td><td>Shopify app install</td><td class="col-good">&#x2713; Good</td><td>Email + chat</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="col-label">Analyzify</td><td class="col-bad">$145&ndash;$275/mo</td><td>Google Analytics 4 focused</td><td>Managed setup</td><td class="col-good">&#x2713; Good</td><td>Dedicated support</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="col-label">Shopify native CAPI</td><td class="col-good">$0</td><td>Meta only</td><td>Data sharing setting</td><td>Shopify does not document what triggers each event</td><td>Shopify Help Centre</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Paid solutions and the Make.com method call the same Meta and TikTok APIs. What the paid ones add is managed setup, a data layer, monitoring and dashboards, and handling for edge cases such as order edits, refunds and subscription renewals. They can also capture the Google click ID in the browser, which a webhook cannot. Make.com&rsquo;s Core plan at $9/month remains far cheaper if your volume outgrows the free credits.</p>
    <p><strong>Our recommendation:</strong> start with what Shopify already provides (Facebook &amp; Instagram data sharing for Meta, enhanced conversions for Google), add the Make.com scenario for TikTok, and evaluate a managed tool when you need refund and edit handling or monitoring you do not want to build.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 8.5: META ONE-CLICK NATIVE CAPI -->
  <div class="sec-mark" id="native-oneclick">META&rsquo;S FREE ONE-CLICK CAPI</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="what-about-metas-new-one-click-conversions-api-april-2026-update">What About Meta&rsquo;s New One-Click Conversions API? (April 2026 Update)</h2>
    <p>In April 2026 Meta launched a Meta-enabled Conversions API: a free, one-click setup inside Events Manager that turns on server-side tracking for Meta with no developer, no code, and no ongoing maintenance. If your only goal is Meta CAPI, this is now the fastest route, and you should use it: it is genuinely easier than building a webhook, and it is free. We would be doing you a disservice not to say so.</p>
    <p>So when does the Make.com method in this guide still make sense? Three honest cases:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>You run TikTok as well.</strong> Meta&rsquo;s one-click setup covers Meta only. The same Make.com webhook can send each order to TikTok&rsquo;s Events API, with the order ID as <code>event_id</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>You want control over the payload.</strong> The native setup is a black box: you get what Meta sends. With Make.com you decide exactly which match keys go out, how IP and user-agent are mapped, and how refunds or order edits are handled. That control decides which match keys Meta receives, and so what your Event Match Quality can be.</li>
      <li><strong>You don&rsquo;t want to depend on each platform&rsquo;s native tool.</strong> Native integrations change on the platform&rsquo;s schedule, not yours. A single Make.com scenario you own keeps your tracking portable if you switch platforms or one native tool degrades.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The honest summary: if you advertise on Meta alone and want the simplest possible setup, use Meta&rsquo;s one-click native CAPI: it&rsquo;s free and excellent for that one job (<a href="/blog/meta-one-click-conversions-api-shopify/">what Meta&rsquo;s one-click Conversions API fixes, and the gaps it leaves</a>). If you also run TikTok, or you want control over the payload and a setup you own, the Make.com method is the free option for that part. A common split is Meta&rsquo;s or Shopify&rsquo;s own route for Meta, the Make.com scenario for TikTok, and enhanced conversions in the Google &amp; YouTube app for Google.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- SECTION 9: TROUBLESHOOTING -->
  <div class="sec-mark" id="troubleshooting">TROUBLESHOOTING</div>
  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="troubleshooting-common-server-side-tracking-issues">Troubleshooting Common Server-Side Tracking Issues</h2>
    <h3 id="events-not-appearing-in-meta-events-manager">Events not appearing in Meta Events Manager</h3>
    <p>Check in this order: (1) Is your Make.com scenario switched <strong>On</strong>? Scenarios default to Off. (2) Did the test order go through checkout and get paid? Use Shopify&rsquo;s test payment gateway if you do not want to take real money; the webhook fires when an order is paid. (3) Is your access token correct and unexpired? Meta access tokens generated via Events Manager do not expire, but tokens generated via the Graph API Explorer expire after one hour. (4) Is your Pixel ID correct? The Pixel ID in your Make.com URL must match the pixel you are viewing in Events Manager.</p>
    <h3 id="double-counting-conversions-seeing-2x-purchases">Double-counting conversions (seeing 2x purchases)</h3>
    <p>Check two things. First, that only one server route sends Meta purchases: if Shopify&rsquo;s Facebook &amp; Instagram app is at Enhanced or Maximum data sharing, turn off the scenario&rsquo;s Meta module. Second, the event IDs: the scenario sends the Shopify order ID as <span class="code-ref">event_id</span>, and Meta and TikTok merge a browser and server purchase only when the IDs match. Shopify does not document its pixels&rsquo; IDs, so if Test Events shows two purchases per order, keep one route and remove the other.</p>
    <h3 id="tiktok-events-show-but-emq-score-is-low-5">TikTok events show but the match score is low</h3>
    <p>A low score usually means missing customer data. Check: (1) Is email being sent and SHA-256 hashed? It must be lowercase before hashing. (2) Is phone in E.164 format (+447911123456) before hashing? (3) Are IP address and user agent mapped from the Shopify order payload? In Make.com, map <span class="code-ref">browser_ip</span> from the order webhook for IP and <span class="code-ref">client_details.user_agent</span> for user agent.</p>
    <h3 id="google-enhanced-conversions-match-rate-below-20">Google enhanced conversions shows no data</h3>
    <p>In the Google &amp; YouTube app, check that enhanced conversions is turned on and the customer data terms accepted, and that the app is connected to the Google Ads account you are looking at. Then check for a second Google Ads purchase tag in your theme, a custom pixel or Google Tag Manager, which can split or double conversions. Diagnostics can take a few days to show data after you turn the setting on.</p>
    <h3 id="makecom-scenario-fails-with-http-400-error">Make.com scenario fails with HTTP 400 error</h3>
    <p>A 400 error from any platform API means your payload is malformed. The most common causes: (1) Missing required fields: check the platform&rsquo;s API docs for required vs optional fields. (2) Incorrect data types: value must be a number, not a string. (3) Invalid timestamp: event_time must be a Unix timestamp (seconds since epoch), not a date string. In Make.com, use <span class="code-ref">formatDate(1.created_at; "X")</span> to send the order time in Unix seconds.</p>
  </div>

